From: ROCKNIX
Subject: [PATCH] usb_intf: stop rtw_dev_shutdown() double-releasing the cfg80211 BSS

With 001 in place rtw_dev_shutdown() finally runs on 6.8+, and that exposed a
latent use-after-free that panics the board on every poweroff, so it reboots
instead of shutting down:

  Internal error: Oops: 0000000096000006 [#1] SMP
  RTW: netdev_vir_if_close(wlan1) , bup=1
  Workqueue: cfg80211 cfg80211_event_work
  pc : cfg80211_put_bss+0x34/0xe4
  Call trace:
   cfg80211_put_bss+0x34/0xe4
   cfg80211_wdev_release_bsses+0x4c/0xc0
   __cfg80211_disconnected+0x5c/0x1cc
   cfg80211_process_wdev_events+0x164/0x180
   cfg80211_event_work+0x2c/0x50
  Kernel panic - not syncing: Oops: Fatal exception in interrupt

Userspace disconnects the interface as the system goes down, queueing one
disconnect event. rtw_hal_deinit() then drops the link and the driver indicates
a second one for the same interface. cfg80211_event_work runs both, each calls
cfg80211_wdev_release_bsses(), and the second cfg80211_put_bss() dereferences a
BSS the first already freed.

rtw_wdev_set_not_indic_disco() is the driver's existing mechanism for exactly
this: it gates only the driver-side indication and leaves the userspace path
alone. Set it on every interface before the teardown starts. The primary
adapter is not enough, because CONFIG_CONCURRENT_MODE gives wlan1 its own wdev
and that is the one in the trace.

The teardown itself is left intact so rtl8733bu-power still finds a quiesced
chip when its own .shutdown drops the enable GPIO.

--- a/os_dep/linux/usb_intf.c
+++ b/os_dep/linux/usb_intf.c
@@ -52,12 +52,30 @@
 	struct dvobj_priv *dvobj = NULL;
 	_adapter *adapter = NULL;
 	struct cmd_priv *pcmdpriv;
+	int i;
 
 	RTW_INFO("%s\n", __func__);
 
 	if (usb_intf) {
 		dvobj = usb_get_intfdata(usb_intf);
 		if (dvobj) {
+			/* rtw_hal_deinit() below drops the link, which makes the driver
+			 * indicate a disconnect for an interface userspace has usually
+			 * already disconnected. Both events queue on cfg80211_event_work
+			 * and each one runs cfg80211_wdev_release_bsses(), so the same
+			 * BSS is released twice and cfg80211_put_bss() faults on the
+			 * freed pointer. The resulting panic reboots the board instead
+			 * of powering it off. Suppressing the driver-side indication
+			 * leaves the userspace one intact. Every interface needs it,
+			 * not just the primary: CONFIG_CONCURRENT_MODE means wlan1 has
+			 * its own wdev and that is the one that faulted.
+			 */
+			for (i = 0; i < dvobj->iface_nums; i++) {
+				if (dvobj->padapters[i])
+					rtw_wdev_set_not_indic_disco(
+						adapter_wdev_data(dvobj->padapters[i]), 1);
+			}
+
 			adapter = dvobj_get_primary_adapter(dvobj);
 			if (adapter) {
 				if (!rtw_is_surprise_removed(adapter)) {
